pfep_description_raw:
The LPD, as well as the other components of the crystalline basement, is dissected by small-scale discontinuities which provide preferential paths for groundwater flow (water-conducting features).  These are classified as:
  •  cataclastic zones;
  •  jointed zones;
  •  fractured aplite/pegmatite dykes and aplitic gneisses.

Irrespective of this classification, water-conducting features consist of sets of sub-parallel, partially infilled fractures.  However, the three classes differ in their geometrical and mineralogical characteristics.  The geometrical characteristics display considerable variability.  On the basis of available information, there is no justification for assuming that the small-scale structure of water-conducting features differs between Area West and Area East.

The conceptual model by which a water-conducting features is represented contains the following elements:
  -  a set of parallel fracture planes within the undisturbed rock (5 in the case of cataclastic zones and 3 in the cases of jointed zones and fractured aplite/pegmatite dykes and aplitic gneisses);
  -  cataclastic matrix (fracture infill) in the fracture planes, containing open channels (representing the flow porosity);
  -  altered wallrock around each fracture;
  -  unaltered wallrock beyond the altered zone.

Examination of water-conducting feature structure leads to the definition of three alternative geometrical representation used in RANCHMD geosphere model (See Table 5.3-1, NTB 93.22):
  -  cataclastic or jointed zones with narrow, closely-spaced channels;
  -  cataclastic or jointed zones with broad, widely-spaced channels;
  -  fractured aplite/pegmalite dykes and aplitic gneisses.

