pfep_description_raw:
As a result of continuing action of geological processes, the future surface geology of Northern Switzerland will be different to the present one.  The effect of certain erosion processes, such as the removal of the Quaternary gravels or denudation (uniform erosion to maximum of 100 m), are not expected to be hydraulically relevant for groundwater flow in the crystalline basement.  This is because hydrostatic pressure at depth is much greater than any likely variations due to changes in topography and infiltration rate.  However, other processes may be more significant and the influence of a range of possible changes, which may occur over the next 106 years, has been investigated using the regional groundwater flow model:
  •  regional uplift of the Black Forest;
  •  the resulting displacement of the river network;
  •  the resulting (linear) erosion of Mesozoic basement cover.

In Area West, with the shift of the regional drainage channel (the river Rhine) to the south and its simultaneous deepening relative to the basement surface, the exfiltration conditions will generally be enhanced, i.e. the upward vertical component of the hydraulic gradient will increase with respect to that of the present day.  However, no fundamental changes in the pattern of groundwater flow are expected.  In Area East, significant changes in the flow regime could occur.  The southward displacement of the Wutach river could bring the area into the catchment of the Wutach, rather than the Rhine river, and into the immediate vicinity of the exfiltration zone. The result would be shorter flow paths and larger hydraulic gradients.

For Area West, the estimated changes in hydraulic gradient fall within the range considered as parameter variations for the LPD within the Reference Scenario.  The possibility of exfiltration to a small valley (e.g. the Wutach) is considered as a variation in the safety-assessment modelling of Area East.

