pfep_description_raw:
The MWCF are dissected by  small-scale discontinuities which provide preferential paths for groundwater flow (water-conducting features).  These are classified as:
  •  cataclastic zones;
  •  jointed zones;
  •  fractured aplite/pegmatite dykes and aplitic gneisses.

Cataclastic zones are predominant within the MWCF.  Irrespective of classification, water-conducting features consist of sets of sub-parallel, partially infilled fractures.  However, the three classes differ in their geometrical and mineralogical characteristics.  The conceptual model, and alternative geometrical representations, by which water-conducting features are represented are identical to that employed for the LPD (see FEP 5.2).

pfep_description:
The MWCF are dissected by  small-scale discontinuities which provide preferential paths for groundwater flow (water-conducting features).  These are classified as:
  •  cataclastic zones;
