Cataclastic zones are predominant within the MWCF.  Irrespective of classification, water-conducting features consist of sets of sub-parallel, partially infilled fractures.  However, the three classes differ in their geometrical and mineralogical characteristics.  The conceptual model, and alternative geometrical representations, by which water-conducting features are represented are identical to that employed for the LPD (see FEP 5.2).

pfep_description_raw:
The mineralogy of the MWCF is heterogeneous.  The average mineralogy of the water-conducting features differs from that of the surrounding rock.  The mineralogy varies: 
(i) according to whether the rock is a granite or a gneiss; 
(ii) according to the type of water-conducting feature; 
(iii) within a water-conducting feature, between fracture infill, altered wallrock and unaltered wallrock; 
(iv) according to the type of alteration, e.g. high-temperature alteration or argillic alteration.  
The mineralogy partially determines the groundwater chemistry (see FEP 6.8).  Both mineralogy and groundwater chemistry influence radionuclide sorption (see FEP 6.9).

The mineralogy of the MWCF is assumed to be unperturbed by the presence of the repository.   Mineralogy is taken into account in the selection of sorption parameter values.

