pfep_description_raw:
ISSUE:  The bottom of the IRUS vault will consist of a 31 cm layer of sand/clinoptilolite (90/10 wt %) on a 30 cm layer of sand/Dochart clay (90/10 wt %).  These buffer layers are designed to sorb nuclides in waste leachate and reduce the escape of these materials from the vault.

Several problems might arise during the emplacement of the buffer that could reduce the effectiveness of the buffer:
-	voids or spaces might be created;
-	the buffer layers might be over-compacted;
-	the two components of each layer might separate;
-	uneven compaction might lead to variability in the hydraulic conductivity of the buffer.

RESPONSE:  Specifications for the controlled preparation and placement of the buffer layers are given in Section 4 of Woods (1995).  Engineered soil barriers beneath LLRW disposal facilities (e.g., the IRUS buffer) are expected to be more reliable than natural soil barriers because of controlled emplacement and their homogeneity (Sandford et al. 1993). 

As per the discussion in FEP 27, buffer emplacement was an issue of greater concern for the performance of the pre-1991 IRUS design. 

PRIORITY B

